---
import Button from "../../components/ui/Button.astro";
import MedicalNote from "../../components/ui/MedicalNote.astro";
import ContentLayout from "../../layouts/ContentLayout.astro";
import ArticleImage from "../../components/ui/ArticleImage.astro";
import type { FaqItem } from "../../lib/seo";

const faqs: FaqItem[] = [
  { q: "What is the Harris-Benedict equation?", a: "The Harris-Benedict equation (1919) was the first formula to estimate BMR from height, weight, age, and sex. It has been largely superseded by Mifflin-St Jeor for better accuracy." },
  { q: "Is Mifflin-St Jeor better than Harris-Benedict?", a: "Research shows Mifflin-St Jeor is 85–90% accurate versus 70–80% for Harris-Benedict. We use Mifflin-St Jeor in our calorie calculator." },
  { q: "What are the BMR equations?", a: "Mifflin-St Jeor: Men BMR=10×weight+6.25×height-5×age+5; Women BMR=10×weight+6.25×height-5×age-161. Harris-Benedict is older and less accurate." },
  { q: "Why was Harris-Benedict replaced?", a: "Harris-Benedict overestimates metabolic rate in modern populations. Mifflin-St Jeor was developed using more recent data and shows better correlation with measured values." },
  { q: "Which BMR equation should I use?", a: "Use Mifflin-St Jeor for everyday calorie planning. It is more accurate and easier to apply than Harris-Benedict. Our <a href="/">BMR</a> uses Mifflin-St Jeor." },
  { q: "Can I use Harris-Benedict if I want?", a: "You can, but expect results that are 200–500 calories higher than Mifflin-St Jeor for the same inputs. This often leads to eating above your true maintenance and gradual weight gain." },
  { q: "Are there BMR equations other than Mifflin-St Jeor and Harris-Benedict?", a: "Yes. Katch-McArdle uses lean body mass and is ideal if you know your body fat percentage. Cunningham is similar but uses a slightly different constant. For most people, Mifflin-St Jeor is the best choice." },
];
---

<ContentLayout
  title="BMR Equations Compared — Mifflin-St Jeor vs Harris-Benedict (2026)"
  h1="BMR Equations Compared"
  description="Compare BMR equations: Mifflin-St Jeor vs Harris-Benedict. Learn which calorie calculator formula is most accurate and why."
  path="/guides/bmr-equations/"
  category="Guides"
  categoryPath="/guides/"
  lead="Two formulas have dominated calorie calculation: Harris-Benedict (1919) and Mifflin-St Jeor (1990). Here's which one to trust."
  updated="June 2026"
  faqs={faqs}
>

  <MedicalNote />

  <p>When you use a <a href="/">Calorie Calculator</a> or any <a href="/">Calorie Calculator</a>, the underlying math matters. Two equations have dominated for decades. Here's how they compare.</p>

  <h2>The Mifflin-St Jeor equation (current standard)</h2>

  <ArticleImage src="/assets/images/articles/resting-flame.svg" alt="Illustration of a flame representing basal metabolic rate and resting energy expenditure." />
  <p>Published in 1990, this formula was developed using modern metabolic data from 247 healthy adults. It outperformed Harris-Benedict by cutting prediction error and better matching measured metabolic rates across diverse populations:</p>
  <table>
    <thead><tr><th scope="col">Equation</th><th scope="col">Formula</th></tr></thead>
    <tbody>
      <tr><td>Men</td><td>BMR = 10 × weight(kg) + 6.25 × height(cm) - 5 × age + 5</td></tr>
      <tr><td>Women</td><td>BMR = 10 × weight(kg) + 6.25 × height(cm) - 5 × age - 161</td></tr>
    </tbody>
  </table>
  <p><a href="/">Calorie Calculator</a> uses this equation exclusively.</p>

  <h2>Deeper dive: Mifflin-St Jeor vs. Harris-Benedict</h2>
  <p>Mifflin-St Jeor is more accurate for several reasons:</p>
  <ul>
    <li>It uses a larger, more diverse sample than Harris-Benedict's original 1919 cohort.</li>
    <li>It was calibrated against indirect calorimetry, a direct measurement of oxygen consumption and carbon dioxide production.</li>
    <li>It simplifies the equation to fewer variables while improving accuracy.</li>
    <li>It has been validated across multiple follow-up studies in different countries and populations.</li>
  </ul>

  <h2>The Harris-Benedict equation (historical)</h2>
  <p>Published in 1919, this was the original formula for estimating metabolic rate. It was groundbreaking for its time but relies on data from a narrow, mostly male, mostly young sample. Modern lifestyle changes — higher body weights, different diets, less manual labor — have made it less accurate for today's population:</p>
  <table>
    <thead><tr><th scope="col">Equation</th><th scope="col">Formula</th></tr></thead>
    <tbody>
      <tr><td>Men</td><td>BMR = 88.362 + 13.397 × weight(kg) + 4.796 × height(cm) - 5.677 × age</td></tr>
      <tr><td>Women</td><td>BMR = 447.593 + 9.247 × weight(kg) + 3.098 × height(cm) - 4.330 × age</td></tr>
    </tbody>
  </table>

  <h2>Revised Harris-Benedict (1984)</h2>
  <p>In 1984, Roza and Shizgal revised the Harris-Benedict equations using updated data. The revised formulas are slightly more accurate than the original but still fall short of Mifflin-St Jeor:</p>
  <table>
    <thead><tr><th scope="col">Equation</th><th scope="col">Formula</th></tr></thead>
    <tbody>
      <tr><td>Men</td><td>BMR = 88.362 + 13.397 × weight(kg) + 4.799 × height(cm) - 5.677 × age</td></tr>
      <tr><td>Women</td><td>BMR = 447.593 + 9.247 × weight(kg) + 3.098 × height(cm) - 4.330 × age</td></tr>
    </tbody>
  </table>

  <h2>Accuracy comparison study results</h2>
  <table>
    <thead><tr><th scope="col">Equation</th><th scope="col">Accuracy vs measured</th><th scope="col">Typical error</th></tr></thead>
    <tbody>
      <tr><td>Mifflin-St Jeor</td><td>~85–90%</td><td>±10–15%</td></tr>
      <tr><td>Harris-Benedict</td><td>~70–80%</td><td>±15–25%</td></tr>
      <tr><td>Revised Harris-Benedict (1984)</td><td>~75–85%</td><td>±12–20%</td></tr>
    </tbody>
  </table>

  <h2>When Mifflin-St Jeor may not be accurate</h2>
  <p>Mifflin-St Jeor is the best general-purpose equation, but there are situations where it may deviate from your true BMR:</p>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>Very lean athletes:</strong> high muscle mass and low body fat can lead to underestimation</li>
    <li><strong>Severe obesity:</strong> adipose tissue is less metabolically active than lean tissue</li>
    <li><strong>Clinical conditions:</strong> thyroid disorders, burns, sepsis, or severe trauma alter metabolism dramatically</li>
    <li><strong>Pregnancy and lactation:</strong> BMR increases significantly; specialized equations exist</li>
    <li><strong>Extreme age:</strong> very young children and very elderly adults may fall outside the validation range</li>
  </ul>
  <p>In these cases, indirect calorimetry in a clinical setting is widely regarded as a reliable method, or you can use an equation that incorporates body fat percentage such as <a href="/guides/katch-mcardle-equation/" class="text-brand hover:underline font-medium">Katch-McArdle</a>.</p>

  <h2>Practical guidance on choosing equations</h2>
  <p>For everyday calorie planning:</p>
  <ul>
    <li>Use <strong>Mifflin-St Jeor</strong> if you do not know your body fat percentage.</li>
    <li>Use <strong><a href="/guides/katch-mcardle-equation/" class="text-brand hover:underline font-medium">Katch-McArdle</a></strong> if you know your body fat percentage and are relatively lean.</li>
    <li>Use <strong>Harris-Benedict</strong> only for historical comparisons or if your clinician specifically requests it.</li>
    <li>Always <strong>verify with real-world tracking</strong> for 2–3 weeks, regardless of which equation you choose.</li>
  </ul>

  <h2>How equations affect your calorie results</h2>
  <p>With similar inputs, the equations often differ by 200–500 calories. This difference matters for weight goals. For example:</p>
  <ul>
    <li>30-year-old woman, 65 kg, 165 cm: Mifflin-St Jeor ~1,400; Harris-Benedict ~1,500</li>
    <li>40-year-old man, 80 kg, 180 cm: Mifflin-St Jeor ~1,820; Harris-Benedict ~2,000</li>
  </ul>

  <h2>Links to calculators and tools</h2>
  <ul>
    <li><a href="/">BMR</a> — uses Mifflin-St Jeor exclusively</li>
    <li><a href="/tdee-calculator/">TDEE calculator</a> — calculates maintenance calories with activity multipliers</li>
    <li><a href="/">Calorie Calculator</a> — splits calories into protein, carbs, and fats</li>
    <li><a href="/">Calorie Calculator</a> — plans your deficit timeline</li>
    <li><a href="/">Weight gain calculator</a> — plans your surplus</li>
  </ul>

  <h2>Best practice: Use Mifflin-St Jeor</h2>
  <p>The scientific consensus favors Mifflin-St Jeor for everyday use. Our <a href="/">calorie calculator</a> uses it, ensuring your <a href="/">maintenance calories</a> and weight goals are based on the highly regarded formula.</p>
<div class='my-10'>
    <div class="my-8 rounded-2xl border border-hairline bg-canvas-soft p-6 text-center sm:p-8">
      <h3 class="mt-0 text-xl font-semibold text-ink">Ready to calculate your needs?</h3>
      <p class="mt-2 mb-6 text-body">Use our free, science-backed calculator to find your exact calorie and macro targets.</p>
      <Button href="/" size="lg" class="w-full sm:w-auto">Open Calorie Calculator</Button>
    </div>
    </div>
</ContentLayout>
